{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"0edc11eac22c6257","type":"text","text":"## GDP\n\nGDP: **Gross domestic product** is the **total value** of all final **products** and **services** produced in a **country** over a **period of time**.\n\n*note: GDP is calculated by recording when money is spent to purchase final product or service, so its basically the total money spent in a country*\n\nGDI: Total **economic output** from **income side**; **aggregate income** earned by all **households**, **businesses** and **government** entities in a **country** over given **period of time**.\n\n*note: exports are captured smoothly by GDI because when foreign customers purchase exports the revenue flows into the domestic economy*\n\n![[Pasted image 20260924173126.png|301]]\n\n### GDP Calculations\n\n#### Income approach\n\nGross domestic income (GDI) should == GDP\n\n![[Pasted image 20260924182655.png|322]]\n\n\n#### Expenditure approach\n\n**GDP = C + I + G + (X-M)**\n\n* Consumer (household spending): **C**\n* Business spending (gross investment): **I**\n* Government spending: **G**\n* Exports (foreign spending on domestic products/services): **X**\n* Imports(domestic spending on foreign products/services): **M**\n\n![[Pasted image 20260927090315.png|463]]\n\n\n### Nominal vs Real GDP\n\n* What we see above is actually nominal GDP\n* Real GDP accounts for price changes without actual output increase (inflation, deflation).\n\n![[Pasted image 20260927091005.png]]","x":-960,"y":-1240,"width":600,"height":440},
		{"id":"c1d68eb3a95ff1ca","type":"text","text":"## Economic Growth\n\n**Economic growth**: **percentage change** in **real GDP** for an economy\n\nGDP growth determined by following:\n\n* **Growth** of the **labour force**\n* **Productivity gains** (growth in output per unit of labour)\n* Availability of capital\n\nDeveloped countries have ageing populations and **low birth rates**, so potential **labour force** grows slowly or **declines**. This means **GDP will grow slowly** **unless** slowing labour force growth is **offset** by **productivity gains**.","x":380,"y":-1240,"width":780,"height":460},
		{"id":"752e0d0c10df0028","type":"text","text":"## Macro Economics\n\nStudy of the economy as a whole, considers the effects of:\n\n* Inflation\n* Economic growth\n* Unemployment\n* Interest rates\n* Exchange rates\n\nCan effect considerations of investment firms\n\n* Some investments benefit from **slow economic growth** and low inflation\n* Some benefit from **fast growth** and moderate inflation\n* Determines which asset classes to invest in","x":-240,"y":-990,"width":500,"height":420},
		{"id":"d33e4b5c61b34786","type":"text","text":"## Business Cycles\n\nBusiness Cycle: Economy wide fluctuations in economic activity.\n\n![[Pasted image 20260927093050.png]]\n\n\n### Expansion\n\n* Production increases\n* Inflation increases\n* High employment - demand higher wages, drives inflation\n* Interest rates start to climb as companies demand credit to leverage growth\n\n### Peak\n\n* Central bank may implement policies to slow economy and control inflation\n* Other factors could include drop in consumer or business confidence caused by:\n\t* Rising oil prices\n\t* geopolitical shocks\n### Contraction\n\n* Real GDP either slows in growth or declines\n* Inflation and interest rates tend to fall due to market forces and central bank actions\n* Unemployment rises\n* Recession classified as negative real GDP for 2 quarters\n\nRecall what **calculates** the **GDP**:\n\n- Consumer spending \n- Business spending \n- Government spending\n- Net exports (exports minus imports)\n\n**changes** in any of these can **stimulate** these **cycles** and they can **amplify eachother..**","x":380,"y":-440,"width":780,"height":620},
		{"id":"896b9cc8616bd0de","x":-1080,"y":-400,"width":720,"height":400,"type":"text","text":"## Economic Indicators\n\nAllow analysis of economic performance and predictions of future performance.\n\nGPD is estimated quarterly and is very hard to get accurately.\n\nIn the United States the Institute for supply managements purchasing managers Index (PMI) is well followed and is a survey sent to businesses covering all NA industry categories to collect information on:\n\n* Production levels\n* New orders\n* Inventories\n* Backlogs\n* Employment\n\n### Leading Indicators\n\nSignal future changes in economy\n\n* Stock market indices\n* Retail sales\n* Building permits\n* Housing starts (new residential construction)\n* Manufacturing activity levels\n\n### Leading Indicators\n\nSignal change in economic activity after output has already changed\n\n* GDP growth\n* Unemployment rate\n* Inflation rate\n* Income and wage growth\n\n### Coincident Indicators\n\nReveal current economic conditions"}
	],
